// The article's policies as a SET, which is how they would actually be deployed.
//
// Each fragment in this directory is vacuous on its own, and that is not a criticism of the
// article -- it publishes them one at a time to explain one idea at a time. A permit gated on
// `get_client_profile::response` cannot fire in a file where nothing permits
// `get_client_profile`: the call is denied, AgentCore records an `::error` rather than a
// `::response`, and the gate never opens. Run any of them alone and Anchor says VACUOUS and names
// the term responsible.
//
// That composition effect is worth seeing once, which is what the single files are for. This file
// is the set with the reads permitted, so the chain can actually run.
//
// TRANSCRIPTION NOTES, so the differences from the published text are not mistaken for findings:
//   - `resource == AgentCore::Gateway::<ARN>` scopes are dropped. Anchor models actions, event
//     kinds and input/output fields; it does not model entity hierarchies, so a resource scope
//     would be noise in the model rather than a constraint on it.
//   - `eventResource: resource` joins go with them, for the same reason.
//   - The budget-cap and single-use-approval policies are NOT here; see README.md for what
//     Anchor refuses and why that refusal is the honest answer.

// ---- the reads, which the article's snippets assume exist ---------------------------------
permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"get_client_profile",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"get_market_price",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"interact_advisor",
    resource
);

// ---- policy 1: workflow sequencing --------------------------------------------------------
permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"load_portfolio",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 5m AgentCore::Action::"get_client_profile"::response{ }
};

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"rebalance_portfolio",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 5m AgentCore::Action::"load_portfolio"::response{ }
};

// ---- policies 2 and 3: the trade gate ------------------------------------------------------
// The article states these as separate permits on the same action. Written that way they are
// alternatives -- either one firing is enough to allow the trade -- which is worth knowing,
// because read as a list of requirements they look like a conjunction.
permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"execute_trade",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 24h AgentCore::Action::"get_client_profile"::response{
        input.profile_id: context.input.profile_id
    }
};

permit (
    principal,
    action == AgentCore::Action::"execute_trade",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 30s AgentCore::Action::"get_market_price"::response{ }
};
